    "answer": {
      "short": "$k=\\pm\\frac12$. The curves meet at $x=0$, where their tangent gradients are $-2k$ and $2k$; their product is $-1$ for either value.",
      "worked": "For $k\\ne0$, the curves meet where\n$$\\begin{align*}k(x-1)^2&=k(x+1)^2\\\\-4kx&=0\\\\x&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nTheir tangent gradients at the intersection are\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=2k(x-1), & g'(x)&=2k(x+1),\\\\f'(0)&=-2k, & g'(0)&=2k.\\end{align*}$$\nFor the tangents to meet at right angles, their gradients have product $-1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}(-2k)(2k)&=-1\\\\-4k^2&=-1\\\\k^2&=\\frac14\\\\k&=\\pm\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $k=0$, the graphs coincide, so it gives no intersection at right angles.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
